<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event</p><h1 class="enc-title">Palapa oath</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1336</span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> · Also known as Sumpah Palapa</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Palapa oath (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sumpah">Sumpah</a> Palapa) is the vow attributed by the Javanese chronicle <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pararaton">Pararaton</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gajah-mada">Gajah Mada</a>, chief minister of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/majapahit-empire">Majapahit</a>, on his appointment around 1336. He swore not to taste palapa, understood as a worldly pleasure, until the islands of the archipelago had been brought under Majapahit. It is celebrated in modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indonesia">Indonesia</a> as a symbol of national unity.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/palapa-oath.jpg" alt="Palapa oath" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Gunawan Kartapranata · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Relief_of_Indonesian_History,_Monas.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
